WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.869
Idea. A customer owes a business four hundred and fifty dollars, then closes down. The money will never come. The business must stop showing it as money owed. This is called writing off the debt.

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:25.954
Every entry has a debit and a credit. To write off a debt, debit the irrecoverable debts account, which is an expense. Credit the customer's account, because they no longer count as owing us.

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:35.913
A provision is different. To create or increase it, debit the income statement and credit the provision account. To decrease it, do the opposite.

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:50.394
Method. A journal entry shows the date, then the account debited first, with its amount in the debit column. The account credited goes on the next line, set a little to the right, with its amount in the credit column.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:01:03.711
So: decide if it is a write-off or a change in the provision. Write the debit account first, then the credit account. Put the amount in both columns. Add a narrative only if the question asks.

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:21.723
Worked example. Here is a real question from June 2024. Shahid's trade receivables include a debt of four hundred and fifty dollars that is irrecoverable. We must prepare a journal entry to write it off. No narrative is needed. Two marks.

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:30.224
First decide. One customer will never pay, so this is a known loss: a write-off. The provision account is not used.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:38.020
Debit first: irrecoverable debts, four hundred and fifty dollars. It is an expense, and expenses are debits.

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:52.945
Then credit the trade receivables ledger control account, four hundred and fifty. Customers now owe less. That is one mark for each line. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:01:54.050 --> 00:02:02.284
Common mistake. A common mistake is reversing the entry. Trade receivables go down, so they are credited, not debited.

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:12.630
Another is crediting the provision when writing off a debt. The provision is only for the estimate. A known loss goes through the irrecoverable debts account.

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:23.433
Your turn. Now you try. Nadia writes off a customer's debt and increases her provision. Write both journal entries. Pause here and work it out.

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:36.405
Answer. Omar's debt is a known loss. Debit irrecoverable debts two hundred and eighty. Credit Omar's account two hundred and eighty, or the trade receivables ledger control account.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:46.045
The provision goes up, so debit the income statement one hundred and twenty, and credit the provision for irrecoverable debts one hundred and twenty.

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:55.706
Remember: decide write-off or provision, debit first, credit below, and the amount in both columns. Now try the practice questions.
